WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.058 --> 00:00:07.600
INTRO:
Do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.720
know who was the first person to&nbsp;
hit a net worth of $200 billion?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.720
If you follow the billionaire’s list closely,&nbsp;
you’d probably guess Jeff Bezos or Elon Musk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.520
But, one financier named Bill Browder has&nbsp;
been claiming that this title actually goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:27.440
to Vladimir Putin who apparently hit the mark&nbsp;
nearly 7 years ago. This is quite ironic given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:33.040
that his official presidential salary is only&nbsp;
8.8 million roubles per year or about $130,000.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:38.720
Even if he were to save his entire salary every&nbsp;
year, it would take him 2 million years to hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:44.960
$200 billion. Even with compound growth of&nbsp;
10% per year, it would take him 125 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.680
But I don’t think any of us are stupid enough&nbsp;
to believe that the presidential compensation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:55.040
is Putin’s true source of wealth. In fact, the&nbsp;
reason that he’s not listed on Forbes or Bloomberg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:04.880
is because he’s believed to have sourced most of&nbsp;
his wealth through illegal avenues. So, here’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.542
how Vladimir Putin managed to accumulate $200&nbsp;
billion and become the modern-day Tsar of Russia.

00:01:09.542 --> 00:01:11.840
GROWING UP IN SOVIET RUSSIA:&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:20.560
Taking a look back, Vladimir Putin was born&nbsp;
on October 7, 1952, in Leningrad, USSR,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:25.200
or modern-day Saint Petersberg, Russia. He was&nbsp;
the youngest of three children born to Vladimir&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.720
Spiridonivich Putin and Maria Ivanovna Putina.&nbsp;
Putin didn’t have much experience getting along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.960
with his siblings though as both of them&nbsp;
had passed away before he was even born.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.360
One of his older brothers, Viktor, had actually&nbsp;
passed away during the siege of Leningrad when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:45.600
the Nazis occupied the city for nearly 3 years.&nbsp;
And Viktor was just one of 1 million civilian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.840
and military casualties during the siege. You&nbsp;
would think that such an experience would leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:55.760
a sour taste in the family’s mouth, but Putin’s&nbsp;
family was actually extremely patriotic. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:00.240
many of his family members served political and&nbsp;
military roles. His grandmother, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.520
was a personal chef for Vladimir Lenin and Joseph&nbsp;
Stalin. Similarly, two of his uncles gave their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.640
lives on the eastern front during World War 2. And&nbsp;
Putin’s father was a conscript in the Soviet Navy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.520
who served until he was severely wounded. Given&nbsp;
that Putin was born well after World War 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.880
he didn’t experience any of these events, but I’m&nbsp;
sure his family told him all about it. Anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.720
Putin would end up attending school No. 193 which&nbsp;
was the local school near his home. At school, one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:31.760
of his favorite tasks was reading books about Karl&nbsp;
Marx and Vladimir Lenin, and as he grew older,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.800
he also started to spend some time learning sambo&nbsp;
and judo. Moving onto high school, Putin would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:40.720
attend Saint Petersburg High School, and one of&nbsp;
the most valuable things he learned there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:49.840
how to speak German. He would later perfect these&nbsp;
skills, and he’s thought to be quite fluent today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.800
Anyway, moving into college, Putin would&nbsp;
spend 5 years in Leningrad State University&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.680
and earned his law degree in 1975. While&nbsp;
at university, Putin became friends with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.400
a business law professor named Anatoly Sobchak,&nbsp;
who will become quite important later on in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:11.840
story. Around the same time, Putin was forced&nbsp;
to join the communist party of the Soviet Union.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:17.040
It’s not clear how exactly Putin felt about this.&nbsp;
When he was a kid, he was one of the only kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:22.080
who didn’t join the Young Pioneer organization&nbsp;
which is exactly what you think it is. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:27.120
Putin would go on to claim that communism is quote&nbsp;
“a blind alley, far away from the mainstream of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:33.040
civilization.” So, I’m not sure if Putin would’ve&nbsp;
joined the communist part out of free will, but he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:38.400
definitely wasn’t going to leave out of free will.&nbsp;
And that brings us into Putin’s days at the KGB.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.560
KGB SPY:&nbsp;

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:47.360
After graduating from law school, Putin&nbsp;
enrolled in the KGB and started his training.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.600
If you’re not familiar with the KGB, it&nbsp;
was the Soviet Union’s intelligence agency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.400
or basically their FBI/CIA. Putin’s&nbsp;
first role after training was within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.560
the Second Chief Directorate which primarily&nbsp;
focused on collecting counterintelligence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.880
This didn’t last for long though as he would soon&nbsp;
be transferred over to the First Chief Directorate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:09.920
where he was tasked with monitoring foreigners and&nbsp;
consulates. Putin completed such menial tasks for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:14.960
nearly a decade until September of 1984 when he&nbsp;
was invited to complete further training at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.840
Yuri Andropov Red Banner Institute. After this&nbsp;
additional training, Putin was sent to Dresden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:25.280
East Germany which is where he got his reputation&nbsp;
for being a badass KGB spy. The story goes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.400
Putin worked as an undercover translator and that&nbsp;
he was involved in crazy events. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.400
one story suggests that Putin helped protect&nbsp;
the integrity of the Soviet Union during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:39.280
collapse of the Berlin Wall. Apparently, he&nbsp;
saved the files of the Soviet Cultural Center,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:44.960
and he burned secret KGB files located in Dresden.&nbsp;
But, many journalists suggest that this is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:50.800
what happened at all. Masha Gessen, for example,&nbsp;
claims that even in Dresden, Putin was mainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:56.160
limited to menial tasks. Masha suggests that Putin&nbsp;
spent most of his time collecting press clippings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.840
most of which were useless to the USSR. Even one&nbsp;
of Putin’s KGB colleagues, Vladimir Usoltsev,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:06.560
agrees that they didn’t accomplish anything too&nbsp;
praiseworthy. Some other journalists suggest that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:11.760
this is just a cover to hide how involved Putin&nbsp;
was with the KGB, but most of the evidence would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:17.280
suggest otherwise. For example, remember how&nbsp;
Putin burned a bunch of files in Dresden? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:21.280
Germany was able to retrieve most of the&nbsp;
important files that he apparently burned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:27.680
Also, it’s not like Putin cleverly left the KGB&nbsp;
right before the collapse of the USSR. The KGB&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:32.480
actually forced him to resign from active service&nbsp;
after Dresden because they felt that they couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:37.360
trust his allegiance. Back in Leningrad,&nbsp;
Putin continued to help with KGB recruiting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:42.240
but as soon as the coup against the Soviet&nbsp;
president began, Putin fully resigned from the KGB&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:47.280
on August 20, 1991, and the USSR would&nbsp;
end up collapsing just a few months later.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:51.280
ENTERING POLITICS:&nbsp;

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:55.040
Now that Putin was out of a job, he decided&nbsp;
to rekindle his friendship with the professor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:59.600
from college, Anatoly Sobchak, and this&nbsp;
couldn’t have been better timing. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:04.400
with the fall of the Soviet Union, Sobchak was&nbsp;
trying to secure a position in politics. Putin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:08.320
helped Sobchak throughout the campaign trail&nbsp;
and he quickly became one of Sobchak’s most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:13.040
trusted allies. Sobchak would go on to win the&nbsp;
election and he became the first democratically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:18.000
elected mayor of St. Petersburg. As gratitude&nbsp;
for all of Putin’s help on the campaign trail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.720
Sobchak would appoint Putin as his advisor on&nbsp;
international affairs. While this was a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:28.000
start for Putin in politics, it didn’t take him&nbsp;
long to leave Sobchak in the dust. On June 28,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.960
1991, Putin was promoted to head of external&nbsp;
relations of the Mayor’s Office, and this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:37.760
when he would spark up his first controversy. It&nbsp;
was revealed that Putin had used his powers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:42.800
approve the export of $93 million worth of metals&nbsp;
in return for foreign food aid that never came.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:47.360
After the news broke, investigators&nbsp;
actually recommended that Putin be fired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:51.520
but this never unfolded because Putin&nbsp;
simply had too much public support.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:56.080
American presidents often struggle to crack&nbsp;
the 50% approval rating threshold. But, Putin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:01.280
has crushed this milestone throughout his entire&nbsp;
career. In fact, even with the Ukraine situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:06.960
Putin’s approval rating currently sits at 71%.&nbsp;
From an outside perspective, this might be a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:12.400
confusing as Putin often comes off as a corrupt&nbsp;
merciless ruler. But most Russians wouldn’t want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:17.760
anyone else in Putin’s place.
RISE TO PREMIERSHIP:&nbsp;

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:21.520
The key to Putin’s popularity&nbsp;
is twofold. The first reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:26.160
is that ever since the Soviet Union dissolved,&nbsp;
the financial situation of the average Russian has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:31.520
exponentially improved. In fact, the average&nbsp;
monthly income has grown nearly 100x. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:34.720
it’s not clear how much of this is&nbsp;
actually due to Putin and how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:39.280
is simply due to a more capitalistic economy,&nbsp;
but a lot of Russians associate Putin’s rise to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:43.760
power with their own financial rise leading them&nbsp;
to pledge their support. The second reason for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:48.240
Putin’s overwhelming support is that he was the&nbsp;
strong leader that many Russians felt they needed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.640
In the 1990s, many Russians felt that their&nbsp;
power and dominance on the world scene had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:58.080
fallen off a cliff ever since the 1960s. And&nbsp;
given Putin’s intense patriotism and iron fist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:02.640
it seemed like he was the right man to return&nbsp;
Russia to its former glory. And if you don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:11.840
believe me, just take a look at 130,000 people&nbsp;
singing the Russian national anthem with Putin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:27.200
Clearly, this is not a guy&nbsp;
that’s lacking public support.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:32.080
Anyway, Putin leveraged this public support&nbsp;
to continue moving up the political ladder.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:36.960
In 1996, he was promoted to deputy chief of the&nbsp;
presidential property management directorate. Two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:41.360
years later, he was promoted to First Deputy Chief&nbsp;
of Staff of the Presidential Executive Office.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:46.400
And in 1999, the president of Russia, Boris&nbsp;
Yeltsin, chose Putin to be the Prime minister of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:52.000
Russia. And when Yeltsin stepped down from being&nbsp;
president, he nominated Putin to be his successor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:55.600
Putin would go on to win the 2000&nbsp;
election garnering 53% of the vote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.600 --> 00:09:01.840
and his popularity has only grown since. For&nbsp;
example, he won the 2018 election with 76.69%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:06.400
of the votes. To put that in perspective, the only&nbsp;
American presidents who won a larger percentage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:11.280
the popular vote were James Monroe and George&nbsp;
Washington. With such overwhelming support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:14.080
it’s clear that Putin isn’t&nbsp;
going anywhere anytime soon.

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:18.080
BILLIONAIRE PUTIN:&nbsp;

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:21.760
So, we’ve covered how Putin got to the&nbsp;
top politically, but how did Putin get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:26.640
to the top financially? After leaving the KGB,&nbsp;
Putin says that he considered becoming a taxi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:30.720
driver to earn extra money, so things&nbsp;
were looking quite bleak. But today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:36.320
he’s potentially the richest person in the world,&nbsp;
so, what happened? Well, like all billionaires,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:40.960
his primary source of wealth is assets starting&nbsp;
with stakes in companies. Putin is speculated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:46.400
have massive stakes in a handful of oil giants.&nbsp;
This includes a 37% stake in Surgutneftegaz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:51.760
a 4.5% stake in Gazprom, and at least a&nbsp;
75% stake in Gunvor. All these companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:56.400
are extremely private and some of them have even&nbsp;
rejected the notion that Putin is a shareholder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:00.800
but critics argue that this is just an attempt&nbsp;
to cover up his involvement with these companies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:05.840
After all, it’s highly unlikely that Putin paid&nbsp;
for these stakes. The more likely scenario is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:10.160
that he was given these stakes in return for&nbsp;
political favors whether that be easier regulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:15.680
or government contracts. Similarly, Putin also has&nbsp;
a vast portfolio of real estate holdings including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:20.800
20 different palaces and villas. And we’re not&nbsp;
talking about real estate worth tens of millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:25.760
or even hundreds of millions. Some of these&nbsp;
holdings individually clock in at over $1 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:30.800
Putin of course denies owning these estates,&nbsp;
and it may be true that he doesn’t directly own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:34.800
these properties. But, it’s speculated that&nbsp;
Putin owns all these stakes and properties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.040
through various proxies. One of Putin’s&nbsp;
political colleagues, Stanislav Belkovsky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:45.280
suggests that Putin is worth at least $40 billion,&nbsp;
and in terms of an upper bound, he has no clue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:50.640
So, Putin is no doubt extremely wealthy, but&nbsp;
he doesn’t really like to show off his wealth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:54.560
It seems that his wealth is more for himself&nbsp;
to enjoy as opposed to something that he shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:59.840
off to the media. Something that Putin is proud&nbsp;
to show off to the world though is his power.

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:03.520
PUTIN TODAY:&nbsp;

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:07.520
By now, I’m sure all of you guys are&nbsp;
familiar with the Ukraine situation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:10.480
It looks like Putin is invading&nbsp;
Ukraine to quote on quote “teach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:11.254
them a lesson.” So far, it doesn’t seem like his&nbsp;
actions have really cost him much public support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.254 --> 00:11:12.640
within Russia. But this could quickly change&nbsp;
if things turn sour. Many western countries are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:17.040
starting to place sanctions on Russia and limiting&nbsp;
their abilities, but I don’t think this really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:21.760
makes a difference given that Putin fully expected&nbsp;
this from the very beginning. In the meantime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:26.800
Putin feels that he’s the wealthiest man not only&nbsp;
in Russia but in the entire world not in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:32.000
of money, though that may also be true, but in&nbsp;
terms of trust. Putin asserts that Russians have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:36.160
entrusted him with the leadership of Russia&nbsp;
for over two decades, and that right there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:40.880
will forever be his greatest wealth. Are you guys&nbsp;
fans of Putin or do you think he’s a douchebag&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:45.920
for invading Ukraine? Comment that down below.&nbsp;
Also, drop a like if you’re a fan of the Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:50.160
national anthem. And of course, consider checking&nbsp;
out our international channels to watch our videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:53.840
in other languages and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

